module gunbc.live_deploy.slice_bounds

import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size }

// ── THE CGROUP BOUNDS THIS DEPLOYMENT'S SERVED PROCESSES RUN UNDER ──────────────────────────
// THEY LIVE IN A LEAF MODULE BECAUSE TWO LAYERS NEED THEM AND ONE OF THEM IS UPSTREAM OF THE
// EMITTER. gunbc.live_deploy.emit renders them into slice units; gunbc.live_deploy.served_slice_charge
// charges them against the host budget, and the budget is reached from the emitter's own import
// closure (emit -> ci_deploy_access -> ... -> ci_runner_placement -> fleet_host_budget). Declaring
// the numbers in the emitter therefore closed an import cycle, which CI's floor caught as
// `circular dependency detected` where the parse sweep could not see it -- acyclicity is the import
// graph's only structural law (DESIGN section 3) and a policy row is not an emission fact.
//
// So the bounds sit where both consumers can reach them and neither can be reached FROM: this
// module imports one std measure module and nothing else.

// THE DEPLOYMENT'S BOUND IS ITS OWN, AND THE SHARED-IDENTITY ARGUMENT THAT MADE IT THE RUNNER
// SLOT'S IS FALSIFIED RATHER THAN OVERRULED (2026-09-12).
//
// WHAT THIS ROW USED TO SAY, and it was true when written: "the bound is the runner slot's, BY
// IDENTITY OF WORKLOAD rather than by coincidence of number -- the deployment's oneshots run
// `gunbc run` over the same corpus a CI job resolves, and the server is the same binary, so
// gunbc_runner_slot_desired is the fleet's one authored allowance for that process and a second
// literal here would be the section-3 fork."
//
// WHAT FALSIFIED IT: the runner row stopped being an allowance for "a gunbc process". The
// 2026-09-12 demand ruling sizes it from the REQUIRED FLOOR'S measured peak -- one prepared subject
// over the whole corpus, 22.74 GiB uncensored -- which is work this deployment never performs. The
// same 2026-09-04 measurement the old note cited for scale is the refutation at this size: serve
// peaked at 3.46 GB and the belt at 0.97 GB. Sharing one number with the floor was a coincidence of
// magnitude that read as an identity of workload, and the moment the floor's number moved, the
// coincidence ended while the sentence asserting the identity did not. That is the pattern this
// corpus keeps finding -- a name that states a coincidence as a law.
//
// THE VALUES DO NOT CHANGE TODAY, deliberately. They are the bytes this deployment already runs
// with, restated as its own fact with its own justification rather than re-derived to something
// tighter in the same change that decouples them. Whether 16 GiB is the right bound for a 4.43 GB
// workload is a separate question with its own evidence; this change only stops the answer being
// decided by the floor's memory demand.
//
// IT ALSO CLOSES A CAP-WRITER BYPASS. `live_deploy` writes this slice with no host admission
// consulted, so while the number was the runner row's, any move to that row propagated to a host
// whose runner allocation may have been REFUSED. Decoupling removes the propagation rather than
// gating it: there is no longer a runner-derived number here to leak.
data live_deploy_slice_memory_max: ByteSize = byte_size(17179869184)

data live_deploy_slice_memory_high: ByteSize = byte_size(16106127360)

// ── THE APPROVAL BROKER'S SLICE AND UNIT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE BOUND IS DERIVED FROM A MEASURED UPPER BOUND, NOT GUESSED AND NOT COPIED. DESIGN section 5's
// oracle rule admits a numeric literal grounded in an explicit policy budget and rejects one
// grounded in a measurement of the current tree, so the basis is recorded beside the policy:
//
//   Before this split, the roadmap serve process served these four routes. Its closure therefore
//   CONTAINED the broker's closure, and that process's resident peak was measured at 3.46 GB on
//   srv1 (2026-09-04, the same measurement live_deploy_slice_memory_max cites). A strict subset of
//   a closure cannot demand more than the whole of it, so 3.46 GB is a sound UPPER BOUND on the
//   broker's cold-load and steady demand rather than an analogy from a similar workload.
//
// The cap is set above that bound with headroom and far below the roadmap's, which is the property
// that matters: the roadmap's demand has no route by which it can reach this slice's allowance.
//
// IT IS AN UPPER BOUND AND THE MODEL SAYS SO. What is NOT established is the broker's OWN demand at
// the six points the split was asked to size against -- cold bind and load peak, idle resident,
// submission-plus-notification peak, confirm-page peak, redemption peak, status-poll peak -- and a
// bound derived from a superset is a ceiling, not a measurement. Stage 2 of
// gunbc.auth.approval_broker_cutover is the instrument that produces those readings against the
// dark deployment, and tightening these two rows from them is that stage's product. Until it runs,
// a ceiling proved by containment is the honest arm, and asserting the tighter number the six
// readings would probably support would be exactly the deduced-from-inferred move DESIGN 4d forbids.
data approval_broker_slice_memory_max: ByteSize = byte_size(6442450944)

data approval_broker_slice_memory_high: ByteSize = byte_size(5368709120)

// MemorySwapMax=0 IS THE RECEIPT'S OWN LESSON WRITTEN INTO THE UNIT. Boot 15 did not fail because
// the roadmap process was large; it failed because that process was SWAPPED and then OOM-killed,
// and a swapped process is one whose latency is unbounded while it still looks alive. An approval
// poll answered after the swap-in is a poll that has already timed out. Denying swap converts that
// into an immediate, attributable kill at a declared bound -- a loud failure with a restart, rather
// than a silent hours-long stall (DESIGN section 5: refuse, never widen).
